sion of a second phase and is associated with strength–toughness balance. [Data: Entities (55, 887, 904, 275); Relationships (51, 1776, 1854, 433)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Multiple mechanisms and condition dependence govern grain refinement\",\"explanation\":\"Grain refinement in steel is achieved under conditions described by Tanaka et al. and can occur through recrystallization; one description specifies static recrystallization rather than dynamic recrystallization, while another describes refinement of austenite grains by recrystallization. In Nb-free steel, refinement influenced by deformation in the two-phase region is attributed to dynamic recovery and/or dynamic recrystallization of α. Deformation in the non-recrystallization region produces grain refinement and reaches a limiting value at 60–70% reduction, while deformation in the two-phase region causes further refinement. It can also be attained by fine dispersion of a second phase and/or by lowering the transformation temperature through addition of substitutional alloying elements. [Data: Entities (55, 887, 904); Relationships (1776, 1854)]\"},{\"summary\":\"A limiting value caps repeated recrystallization and yields coarse α-grains\",\"explanation\":\"Repeated recrystallization does not proceed indefinitely; grain refinement through repeated recrystallization reaches a limiting value and results in relatively coarse α-grains. The limiting value represents the ceiling recrystallized grain size attained as reduction increases. Although repeated recrystallization normally cannot refine grains beyond this point, the limiting value can be exceeded by dividing the γ-grain. [Data: Entities (275, 290); Relationships (433, 455)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Grain refining improves both strength and toughness\",\"explanation\":\"GRAIN REFINING is a process that produces a grain-refining effect used by controlled rolling. It is a way of increasing strength and improving toughness by refining grains, contributing to both properties. Grain-refining elements are most desirable because grain refining enhances both strength and toughness; relationships show grain refining increases strength and grain-refining elements enhance strength. [Data: Entities (902, 908, 55); Relationships (1847, 1867, 51)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Hardening mechanisms influence hardness and transition temperature\",\"explanation\":\"HARDENING refers to the increase in hardness of a material. In steel, hardening can be caused by grain refinement, solid solution, precipitation, or transformation. Hardening due to precipitation, dislocations, and related mechanisms is one of the factors that determine transition temperature. Thus hardening encompasses multiple microstructural mechanisms and can influence both hardness and transition-temperature behavior. [Data: Entities (959); Relationships (2610)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Toughness is optimized by processing and composition\",\"explanation\":\"Toughness is a mechanical property of steel, especially controlled-rolled steels, enhanced by rolling passes and controlled-rolling routes. Improvements arise from grain refinement, fine ferrite grain size, Nb additions, and finish rolling at lower temperatures, especially for Nb steel; heavy deformation in the two-phase/intercritical region also improves toughness. Conversely, Ti addition, coarse grain structures, upper bainite and/or coarse ferrite grains, and bainitic structures decrease toughness. Nb-free steel is used as a comparison for toughness improvement after finish rolling at lower temperatures. Toughness is represented in terms of normal stress acting on the cleavage surface. [Data: Entities (7, 113); Relationships (122, 1324)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Low Nb content and second-phase routes link composition to refinement\",\"explanation\":\"In low Nb content conditions, major hardening is due to grain refinement. Grain refinement can also be attained by fine dispersion of a second phase, linking SECOND PHASE and FINE DISPERSION to the centr